3	// This pass lowers the julia.cancellation_point intrinsic to:
4	// 1. A stack buffer allocation for a jl_reset_ctx_t
5	// 2. Saves of the task's gcstack and eh into the buffer
6	// 3. A setjmp call on the buffer's mctx field
7	// 4. Assignment of the buffer address to task->reset_ctx (atomic release)
8	//
9	// It also walks the function to find stores/calls without julia.reset_safe
10	// metadata and inserts reset_ctx = NULL before them.
11	//
12	// Additionally, the pass runs on every function codegen marked with the
13	// julia.ipo_reset_safe attribute (its own IPO effects are reset_safe - the
14	// same condition under which call sites to it may be tagged), even when it
15	// contains no cancellation point itself: such a function may execute inside
16	// a caller's reset region that was kept open across a reset_safe call.
17	// Machinery the runtime inserts implicitly (runtime library calls, dynamic
18	// dispatch) is not part of the IPO contract that justified the tag - its
19	// frames (malloc, exception setup, the JIT) must never be abandoned by a
20	// delivered reset - so every such call (and every call not itself tagged
21	// reset_safe) eagerly drops the published reset context; the caller's next
22	// cancellation point re-establishes the region (delivery is
23	// level-triggered). Allocations and write barriers are the exception: the
24	// pass annotates each such site that may execute with a region published
25	// (julia.reset_region metadata), and FinalLowerGC lowers those sites to
26	// *_reset_safe runtime entry points that unpublish/republish the region
27	// themselves, so they need no per-site drop and the region even survives
28	// them. Functions that are not reset_safe need no instrumentation: no reset
29	// region can be open while they run.
